### Introduction

Put `\<...>` right after a Type name, instead of on its own, and you get something different from a plain Struct value: an independent variant of that type -- its own `new`, its own methods, completely separate from the bare type and from every other variant. This file assumes you already know what a Struct is -- see demos/structs.prog first if `<...>` alone is new to you.
###
String\<Dictionary> { to_s (; "I'm a dict-tagged string" ) }
String\<Number>     { to_s (; "I'm a number-tagged string" ) }

@assert String\<{x=1}>().to_s() == "I'm a dict-tagged string", '{x=1} is a Dictionary, so it should match the Dictionary-tagged variant'
@assert String\<5>().to_s() == "I'm a number-tagged string", '5 is a Number, so it should match the Number-tagged variant'

# Matching isn't exact-name-only: a value satisfies a member if its own type composes what's
# declared, not just if it IS that type by name. No matching variant raises Undeclared_Type_Structure.


### Disambiguating variants by name

When two variants would otherwise match by type alone, name the member at the reference site (reusing the same bare `:=` idiom a plain Struct literal uses) to disambiguate:
###
Table_With_Opts\<dict: Dictionary>  { to_s (; "dict-named" ) }
Table_With_Opts\<other: Dictionary> { to_s (; "other-named" ) }

@assert Table_With_Opts\<dict := {x=1}>().to_s() == 'dict-named', 'naming the member at the reference site should pick the "dict" variant'
@assert Table_With_Opts\<other := {x=1}>().to_s() == 'other-named', 'naming the member at the reference site should pick the "other" variant'


### Reference vs. declaration

`<...>` after a Type name means something different depending on what follows it:

  Abc\<Number> {}      -- declaration: defines a new variant
  x := Abc\<Number>    -- reference: dups the matched variant, doesn't mutate it
  Abc\<Number>()       -- construct: binds .tag onto the instance BEFORE Self(;) runs

A tagged reference is a dup of the variant it matched, so tagging it can never leak back and mutate the shared declaration (or any other reference sharing that variant). `.tag` is readable inside `Self(;)` itself, but member values are never forwarded as constructor arguments -- whatever you pass to `(...)` still just binds to new's own declared params, entirely separately.
###
Abc\<Number> {
	Self (;)
}
z  := Abc\<4815>
zz := z()
@assert zz.tag.@types.first() == 4815, '.tag should carry the value the reference was tagged with'


### Reusing a struct: the spread rule

A lone, unnamed member whose value is itself a Struct gets spread when declaring a variant -- its own members become the declaration's own members, instead of nesting as one opaque value.
###
DEFAULT_COLUMNS := <id: Number, created_at: Number>

Thing\<DEFAULT_COLUMNS> {}   # same as writing: Thing\<id: Number, created_at: Number> {}

t := Thing\<5, 1234>()
@assert t.tag.id == 5, 'a spread member should land on the declaration by its own name'
@assert t.tag.created_at == 1234, 'a spread member should land on the declaration by its own name'


### References never spread

The spread rule above only applies when declaring -- a reference never spreads. A bare reference to a Struct-valued identifier is always treated as ONE opaque value, exactly like a reference to any other type:
###
Options := <a: String, b: Number>

Widget\<opts: Options> {
	Self (;)
}

Widget\Options {}

widget_a := Widget\<opts: Options>()   # named reference, matches the declared "opts: Options" variant -- works
widget_b := Widget\<Options>()         # bare reference -- Options treated as one opaque value, NOT spread -- also works
widget_c := Widget\Options()
@assert widget_a.tag.@names == ['opts'], 'a named reference should still carry the member name from the matched variant'
@assert widget_b.tag.@types.first() == Options, 'a bare reference should hold Options as one opaque value, not spread'
@assert widget_c.tag.@types.first() == String, 'the first type inside this tag is a: String'
